# evidence-timeout: 300
#
# D-133 -- two residual holes in `_escalation_overrides` and the common root
# underneath them. D-101's fix is correct and closed: a MENTION of the token is
# no longer a REQUEST, in scoped/wildcard/bare/=/indented/uppercase/
# trailing-period form. What survived it, both "invisible rather than refused":
#
# (1) THE VALUE. `_OVERRIDE_SCOPED_RE`'s group was `(\S+)`, so a class key
#     containing a SPACE could never be overridden -- and FR-007 makes `class`
#     free text that a stream writes. Driven: "SHARED RESOURCE LEAK" escalates;
#     Foundry-Directive("escalation-override: SHARED RESOURCE LEAK") returns
#     ok:true "injected", `_escalation_overrides()` is set(), and the class
#     stays escalated. Worse than inert: `_structural_proposal` interpolates
#     that key into the instruction it hands the lead, so the tool told the
#     operator to send a string it could not read back. The fix is not just a
#     wider group -- `_override_instruction` now RENDERS the marker and
#     verifies it against the reader, so what this server prints is always
#     something this server honours.
#
# (2) THE PREFIX. `^[\s>]*` admitted the blockquote character, so QUOTING an
#     escalation packet into a directive -- "> escalation-override: X", even
#     buried at line 8 of a 9-line note -- silently de-escalated the class. A
#     quotation reports what someone else wrote; it is never a request. The
#     quoted form is still MATCHED, by its own pattern, so it can be reported
#     as ignored instead of vanishing.
#
# (3) THE ROOT, and the reason the other two stayed invisible for a cycle:
#     NOTHING REPORTED AN OVERRIDE IN EITHER DIRECTION. Foundry-Directive
#     returned ok:true "Directive injected" naming no recognised override, and
#     the consumer silently `continue`d past the class it dropped. The last
#     table drives all five outcomes -- no directive, a working override, a
#     mistyped key, a space-carrying key, a quoted marker -- and counts the
#     distinct reports: 1 of 5 before, 5 of 5 now. An operator could not tell a
#     live override from a dead one except by watching what the next
#     Foundry-Tasks emitted, which is the shape of every defect in this class.
#
# NFR-002 for the grammar itself. Widening the value group to end-of-line is
# exactly the direction that could resurrect D-101's inversion, so all seven
# phrasings D-101 closed are re-driven against the new grammar (third table),
# and the five forms AC-010 requires are re-driven too (fourth table).
